# Architecture

`{{ project_name }}` combines a Python CLI with Snakemake workflows
and Quarto documentation. This document maps each directory to its
purpose so you can navigate the codebase without guessing. Note that
some directories are optional and may be added by specific templates
after project initialisation.

## Repository root

Top-level files that configure the project as a whole.

- `README.md` -- Project overview and quick-start install instructions
  for end users.
- `CONTRIBUTING.md` -- Developer setup, dependency management, and
  guidance on extending the project with `nbis-admin`.
- `pyproject.toml` -- Python package metadata, dependencies (managed
  via `pixi`), and CLI entry points.
- `pixi.lock` -- Locked, cross-platform environment specification.
  Commit this; do not edit by hand.
- `{{ project_name }}.yaml` -- Project-specific configuration read by
  the `{{ project_name }}` CLI at runtime. Edit this to change
  project-level settings such as Snakemake profiles and documentation
  configuration. See [Configuration lookup](#configuration-lookup).
- `.gitignore`, `.pre-commit-config.yaml`, `.markdownlint.yaml`,
  `.editorconfig`, `.prettierrc.yml` — Tooling configuration for
  version control, code style, and formatting.

## Configuration lookup

The CLI looks for its configuration file in this order:

1. the `--config-file` option
2. the `{{ python_module.upper() }}_CONFIG_FILE` environment variable
3. `{{ project_name }}.yaml` in the current directory
4. `$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/{{ project_name }}/{{ project_name }}.yaml`
   (`~/.config` if `XDG_CONFIG_HOME` is unset)

If no file is found, the CLI warns and uses default settings.

With options 1 and 2, the directory containing the file becomes the
project home, and Snakemake runs there (`--directory`). With 3 and 4
the project home is the current directory. A workflow can therefore
be run from anywhere:

    {{ python_module.upper() }}_CONFIG_FILE=/path/to/{{ project_name }}.yaml \
        {{ project_name }} smk main run

The snakemake working directory can also be set with the environment
variable {{ python_module.upper() }}_SNAKEMAKE_DIRECTORY.
Consequently, separate analyses can live in an `analysis` subdirectory
or in a completely different location, as long as the pixi environment
is activated.

For improved reproducibility, you can add an .envrc with environment
variables {{ python_module.upper() }}_CONFIG_FILE and
{{ python_module.upper() }}_SNAKEMAKE_DIRECTORY set. In addition,
set SNAKEMAKE_CONDA_PREFIX to point to the root project directory,
allowing for reusable conda environments.

## `data`

Project data, organised freely. Some recommendations:

- `data/external` -- for external data sources such as annotation
  files and sequence references
- `data/interim` -- for transient data

## `docs`

Project documentation files in markdown (`.md`) or Quarto (`.qmd`)
format.

- `docs/diary` -- diary in HTML format
- `docs/running_slides` -- running slides in Revealjs format
- `docs/assets` -- document assets

## `results`

Main project results. Free organization.

## `src/{{ python_module }}`

Main entry point for Python source code that operates the CLI and
subcommands. The subcommands live in `commands` and can be listed by
typing:

    {{ project_name }}

The `smk` subcommand is defined in `commands/smk/__init__.py`, with
one file per workflow group (for example `commands/smk/main.py`). It
provides wrappers to access individual workflows (see next section):

    {{ project_name }} smk main run

This command runs workflow `main` defined in
`src/{{ python_module }}/workflow/smk/commands/main/run.smk`

## `src/{{ python_module }}/workflow/smk`

Snakemake workflow files, organised following best practices.

## Workflow-related directories

- `.snakemake` -- Snakemake run history and cluster logs
- `benchmarks` -- Snakemake benchmarks
- `config` -- Snakemake and Nextflow configuration files.
- `logs` -- Snakemake logs
- `notebooks` -- Notebooks (e.g., Jupyter) for one-shot analyses.
- `resources` -- sample sheets and sample set definition files.
